{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's workflow MUST end by naming the bundled skill for the next task, or, when different outcomes lead to different tasks, the skill for each such outcome. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.next` is a distributed requirement. Each bundled skill's workflow must end by naming the skill for the next task. If different outcomes lead to different tasks, it must name the skill for each. An outcome that leads to no further task needs no skill named. Each site upholds this on its own. The skills are also checked by the project test below, which I assessed.\n\n**Site query `skills`.** `run_named_query` returned exactly five sites. They are the `SKILL.md` files for `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. A search for `^name: ` found these same five files with skill front matter. The only other matches were CI workflow files, which are outside the scope. The reference files under `assets/skills/*/references` have no skill front matter and are rightly not selected. The query has a match snippet with front matter and a no_match snippet without it, which fits the front-matter-recognized scope. I found no missed place and no place selected outside the scope.\n\n**Test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_workflows_end_by_naming_the_skill_for_the_next_task]`.**\n- Each skill's last workflow step is taken from `workflow_steps(name).pop()`. `assert_text` then asserts specific phrases in it.\n- `habeas` is asserted to contain \"Follow that skill\". Its step 2 reads \"Follow that skill; each one names the next\". The test also asserts that the routing table in `habeas` step 1 has a `| name |` row for every other skill. Together these show that `habeas` names the next skill for each task.\n- `habeas-requirements` is asserted to contain \"Next, follow the habeas-evidence skill\". This matches its step 7.\n- `habeas-evidence` is asserted to name `habeas-requirements` for findings that need code, tests or requirement text changes. This matches its step 7. The other outcome, no findings, needs no skill.\n- `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` is asserted to name `habeas-requirements` for gap-list items the user decides. This matches its step 9.\n- `habeas-refactor` has two outcomes, and both phrases are asserted. One names `habeas-evidence` after the reassessment. The other names `habeas-requirements` for later behavior or text changes. This covers the multi-outcome clause.\n\nThe assertions fail if a skill's final step loses its named skill. All five skills are covered by the list, which matches the `SKILLS` constant. The \"no further task needs no skill\" clause is a permission, so it needs no test.\n\n**Gaps.** None established. I did not see a violation query in the inputs, so I have nothing to note about one.",
  "references": "cad77bef8fd9a6e827e81314c255639bcbcc74e3c865da250136997ac253fa61",
